  instrument: book15Instrument,
  editorialNote: "The three journeys, twenty-four reading sections, visual models and diagnostic are editorial learning aids. The sections follow Ghazali's three chapters in his order, and nothing in them is summarised away: each of the eight rights is given with the reports, verses and stories he gathers for it, and the third chapter — on every Muslim, neighbours, kin, parents, children and those in one's power — is rendered in full, including its many scenes of the Prophet's own conduct. The English is an original synthesis made from a reading of the public Arabic text, not a translation and not a substitute for one. Reports and inherited stories are presented as material Ghazali passed on; this edition does not independently grade every narration. The last section concerns slaves and servants in the social order of Ghazali's time, and is given as he wrote it; its governing principle — that God has more power over you than you have over anyone in your power — is stated by the Prophet in the chapter itself. The diagnostic applies his own framing and his own eight rights to a friendship the reader supplies, and cannot pronounce on anyone's relationships."
